<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The global interest rate rising cycle has =
spun again today, after the <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian=
.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEDZQCxC_19XC5K9o6Gu=
ywi2zM1QBBWXQxqEeHJ1UrINQ5snixeJYeTnE1ZPv5NfM0NcMOhhmQLUFM_AVVgM4T4uv2D2GqT=
tq33jyBgoXlOPv2Naujl3Bai17TpKFEmP8qQoSzWxwrCAuOHTV8IaaSklutRcw40_4aLTi_NFKa=
GLREWCxARHVQvxCHOleoO-mbv6kxeCjZKjS_hHvFJDK0Jx/4u5/OO9D8ghQTXOQ--MrQQrRXA/h=
30/h001.goDFhUNftYSjpTJyqFQ-n2FkgtD0OYsB0AmFFA6Equc" style=3D"color: #0077B=
6;">Bank of Japan</a> decided to raise interest rates to their highest leve=
l in 31 years.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The BoJ voted to raise its target interest=
 rate by a quarter of one percentage point to 1.25%, the highest level sinc=
e 1995. The vote was not unanimous =E2=80=93 with two board members dissent=
ing to the increase.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The move meant the BoJ has joined the US F=
ederal Reserve and the European Central Bank in tightening monetary policy =
this month, as part of the global fight against inflation.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">However, the Bank of England is, so far, r=
esisting joining the battle, having yesterday voted to leave UK interest ra=
tes on hold at 3.75%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The BoJ has been in a rate-rising cycle si=
nce 2024, when it lifted its policy rate out of negative territory. It has =
been under pressure to raise borrowing costs as the yen weakened steadily a=
gainst the dollar this year, to levels that prompted policymakers to interv=
ene to stabilise the currency.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">A rise today had been expected, so the new=
s that two BoJ policymakers opposed the move has excited the markets.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Despite the inflationary squeeze on househ=
olds, retail sales across Great Britain have risen over the summer.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The heatwave, a pickup in web shopping and=
 the joys (and <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u00=
1.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgIKs-YhPJt=
P0VpWomMzd4gGgOFzmgKpT8Tc33gXGeVEyS-tX7IewtT7htqpBBiUHfVfU-0lqkiKyXfE2tZXds=
4wc-MHvIEc3zl61vVqv5tFeEmcCKjmV4tj6OK-f7JzPEUDMChSUCj5Iz4DdemGdfy0-xKndD630=
sM-Y1zH3pJwA34qpXHsBtb5xLAT5WsTJPgUDg2eKtHHe0WXNdONFUsell4ShvMMUZuUgEb9O5Db=
t7K_98z961ifBajNQEV9u3/4u5/OO9D8ghQTXOQ--MrQQrRXA/h31/h001.Ce_NnZI8whvjVDNI=
4vilmxD-P9ZIHOk67dkClUPFHVU" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">pain </a>and <a href=
=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18I=
RyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPg0rHAjP8PpSJU3jz-hAD5hv1p_Evc9ds=
pzfTQIjBHFc-TLyCqc1mY5MlOjWZB9gtZG-F64XdV1TQIlo0KI33X4v7YnDbnRr7VPoTFdLFvBZ=
DwILUgMORsMGQ6FjDHYOc6kvdHE1Uwvf__xfLB6iHqRfQX6BbZkwOCsqjBRKmseFuQLewYJpzJb=
notSay06BabR0HxfpFnv7z1D4mmQX_suqTcACibNmmqVkDkIXbvswA/4u5/OO9D8ghQTXOQ--Mr=
QQrRXA/h32/h001.tqTLOLG35Nop7wsjurDC-qb1YV4D2Uc4MDgqCDYqaWw" style=3D"color=
: #0077B6;">disappointment</a>) of the men=E2=80=99s football World Cup, he=
lped to lift spending over the three months to August, data shows.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Retail sales volumes rose by 0.9% in the J=
une-August quarter, the Office for National Statistics has reported this mo=
rning.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Non-store retailers=E2=80=99 sales volumes=
 rose after a particularly strong June period =E2=80=93 perhaps because peo=
ple preferred to order goods online rather than braving the high street in =
the heatwave.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Retailers selling alcohol and beverages pe=
rformed well across all three months to August, which they attributed to pr=
omotions, the hot weather and the World Cup.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In August alone, retail sales volumes rose=
 by 0.5%, reversing a 0.5% drop in July.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">August=E2=80=99s rise is unexpected (econo=
mists had forecast a 0.2% fall), so this is the latest piece of economic da=
ta to beat expectations after <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardi=
an.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFuifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEAsxWg7d8sWyUjf1C=
JIvNP3df5a-dcFDOn56S8Qh9nWcsR5Oeg3GrYMi5S2YwEfgr69GTN__SZ0_KRkDLvFn_6ixIsLP=
ifX2CZ4_v0ha6fQRYl3LqPrz2UlyDE5lDFddX4acOj8cTCunE6M1cl5u-a627jOaRhx9nN5GcXq=
gLjaoo_7OwKXQ4_jZGO0L1JLhGrzBlNjVlaBtrn1GhJSBgCyX_JPJ4G0ypusZik_Zg-ldM0Gru2=
tGvsfRrzNEYcSuOjstqt71dKT2W1CWuQC8WgM/4u5/OO9D8ghQTXOQ--MrQQrRXA/h33/h001.n=
mca0OQcPxeSS-qBzh7gNKpQJG6iDAjk9wMM-SPz2OI" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">last =
week=E2=80=99s jump in UK GDP</a>.</p>
